    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** (74.41) stands as our premier offensive anchor. Facing Ure\u00f1a, who carries a shaky 4.42 xERA, **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** benefits from a massive 10% SP skill edge and a high-leverage #3 spot in the lineup. Joining him in the elite tier are **Lawrence Butler** (70.88) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (66.68). **Lawrence Butler** is particularly attractive due to a 13% platoon advantage and a +10% park factor, while **Jung Hoo Lee** leverages a strong wind-out environment and a favorable matchup against Singer to maintain his efficiency. We are also leaning on **Jonathan Aranda** (68.94) today, as his 12% platoon edge against Jobe makes him a mandatory start in this format.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench involved **Gleyber Torres** (50.33), who was under consideration for a middle infield or utility spot. While his #1 batting order assignment provides a substantial boost, his projected efficiency fell just shy of our active threshold when weighed against the upside of our starting outfielders. Meanwhile, we continue to monitor **JJ Bleday** (54.40); while his score remains in the safe zone, the 14% penalty from his platoon matchup against Houser is a concern, though the favorable wind conditions keep him firmly in the lineup.\n\nRisk management remains a priority given our 5x5 Roto constraints. We are closely watching the performance of **Zack Gelof** (57.37), who receives a subtle bump (+2%) from the opposing pitcher's slow delivery, a factor we heavily weight due to the premium on stolen bases. Conversely, our catchers **Yainer Diaz** (51.65) and **Cal Raleigh** (49.95) are hovering near the floor of our starting criteria. Both are active today, but their low position in the order and tough matchups against Warren and Nola respectively mean they are candidates to be benched if we need to preserve games caps later in the week. Ensure your settings are locked and keep an eye on **Dylan Beavers** and **Braden Montgomery**, who remain on the bench as low-efficiency options for today\u2019s rotation.",
